WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.835
PRIYANKA VERGARDIA:
Hey, it's Priyanka.

00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:04.170
So far, we've looked at
how Critter Junction was

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:07.320
able to launch a new
app on Google Cloud.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.180
Today, let's take
a look at how they

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.820
can enable their apps
to gracefully handle

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.919
peaks and dips in traffic.

00:00:13.919 --> 00:00:15.875
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:20.765 --> 00:00:24.400
Critter Junction is becoming
very popular with more users

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:25.450
than ever.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:28.840
The game is all about playing
daily, collecting items

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.210
and furniture to
decorate your house,

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.550
and interacting
with other players.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.320
As we saw in the
previous episode,

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.210
they have a range
of compute options

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.250
to deploy their
workloads, but they

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:43.000
chose to migrate some game
service to Compute Engine.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.220
As their traffic grew,
they were struggling

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.180
to provision
additional instances

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.570
globally for anytime of the day.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:53.560
This led to overutilized Compute
and created constant pressure

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.050
on their operations teams.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:58.060
Clearly, they need
an automated way

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:01.240
to handle their growing users
and maintain performance

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.489
to keep their users
coming back daily.

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:07.840
So let's see what Critter
Junction should do.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.940
They should use a
global load balancer

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.880
and managed instance groups
to scale and distribute

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.800
the traffic automatically.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.140
This keeps the
operations team happy

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:20.730
and the user satisfied
with great performance.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:24.220
Managed instance groups provide
features such as autoscaling,

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:28.330
autohealing, auto updating,
and regional multiple zone

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:29.800
deployments.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.080
To understand this
better, let's step back

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.070
and understand how a Compute
Engine Instance is created.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:39.700
You create a custom image for
your application, which is then

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.330
used to create an instance,
and to make this reusable,

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:45.550
you create an instance template.

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:47.890
In an instance template,
you can not only

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.800
set up configuration
of the VM, but also

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.760
run startup scripts to pull down
the latest version of your code

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.710
when the machine starts up.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.740
You can also attach disk
templates with all the software

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.000
dependencies your app requires.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.390
Or you leave it
as an empty shell

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.630
that gets populated
by a CICD pipeline.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:10.090
These templates then
automate the creation

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.700
of Compute Engine
instances at scale

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.320
through managed instance groups.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:19.150
Let's see how this works with
a simple web app example.

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.120
Let's head over to our project
in the Google Cloud Console

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:25.270
and create a firewall
rule under VPC networks

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:29.460
to allow HTTP traffic to the
app we're about to deploy.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.100
Provide a name,
default-allow-http,

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:37.960
default network, targets,
select the specific target tags,

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:41.950
and set the target
tag as http-server.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:48.370
Set the source filter to IP
ranges and provide 0.0.0.0/0

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:51.470
to allow access for
all IP addresses.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:55.270
For ports and protocol,
select TCP and enter 80.

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:57.790
And then Create.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:59.860
Now, head over to
the Compute Engine

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.080
and create an instance template.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:06.100
Give it a name, select machine
type, set boot disk image to

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:10.060
[INAUDIBLE],, and check
Allow HTTP Traffic.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.670
Under the Management
tab, find automation

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.870
and add to startup script.

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:18.340
This script causes each
instance to run a simple web

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:23.080
application during startup,
and finally, Create.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.540
Link to the tutorial is
included below for you

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.850
to refer to the sample script.

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:29.780
Now that we have an
instance template,

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:33.040
we can create an instance
group using this template.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:34.540
Create an instance group.

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:35.600
Give it a name.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:38.110
Select multiple
zones under location.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:40.540
This protects us
from zonal failures.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:43.280
Select a region, and
under Instance Template,

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.130
select a template
we just created.

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:49.270
Now, set autoscaling
mode to Autoscale,

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:52.270
autoscaling policy
to CPU utilization.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:56.290
You can also set policy to HTTP
load balancing or monitoring

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:57.180
metrics.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.080
We're going to talk
more about HTTP load

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:00.640
balancing in a minute.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.840
We will set the target CPU
usage to 60%, minimum number

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.760
of instances to 3.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:09.940
It is recommended that you
provision enough instances so

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:12.610
that if an entire
zone was to go down,

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:14.560
the remaining
instances still meet

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:16.579
the minimum number required.

00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:19.510
And set the max number
of instances to 6

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:22.900
to make sure you don't
incur additional cost.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:26.200
We will set the cool down
period to 120 seconds.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.300
Make this number is
higher than the time

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:32.820
it takes for CPU utilization of
the VM to initially stabilize.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.200
We will skip setting a
health check for now,

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.780
but we'll cover that
in the next episode.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:39.910
After that, just Create.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:43.480
Wait for a few minutes until all
of the instances are running,

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.850
then go to VM
instances, and click

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:48.160
on the external
IP of the instance

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.480
to see the demo web page.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.510
Now that we have it
all set up, let's

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:56.380
generate traffic so we can
see the autoscaling in action.

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:57.700
Open Cloud Shell.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:01.630
Create a local bash project
using export project ID

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:02.550
command.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:03.950
Run this bash script.

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:06.520
Again, that's included
in the link below.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.470
This script
increases load, which

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:12.250
leads to increase in CPU
utilization for our demo app.

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:15.190
When it reaches the
target value of 60%,

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:17.200
the autoscaling
starts increasing

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:18.810
the size of our instance.

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:21.930
Now, let's navigate
to our Monitoring tab

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:23.580
in our instance
group, and we can

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:25.890
see the increasing
number of instances

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:28.530
as the CPU usage is increasing.

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:30.600
And you can see the
scaled down effect

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.700
by running a similar
bash script that

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:37.230
decreases the load, leading to
a decrease in CPU utilization.

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:39.990
And after a few minutes
of stabilization period,

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:42.990
the autoscaler prompts to
decrease the instance group

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:47.370
size, which is visible in
the monitoring tab as well.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.410
Critter Junction
has global users.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:51.210
They want the users
from Singapore

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:55.050
to end up on Asia East web
server while the ones in the US

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:57.180
end up on US Central Region.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:00.180
For this, they would use
global load balancing,

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.880
which routes traffic to
the nearest web server

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.240
instance, which helps
reduce latency and improve

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:07.320
performance.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.150
From there, the
internal load balancer

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:12.060
distributes the traffic to
manage and maintain load

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:13.830
across the back ends.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:16.290
These instance groups
in different regions

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:18.960
autoscale using an
HTTP load balancing

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:22.380
policy to scale seamlessly,
regardless of where

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:24.870
the traffic is coming from.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:27.750
Not only was Critter
Junction able to automate

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:29.880
the scaling of their
compute instances

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.790
using autoscaling and
managed instance groups,

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:36.060
they were also able to improve
performance by serving traffic

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:39.870
from instances closest to their
users using the global load

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:41.310
balancing.

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:44.580
But there's one
more step to scale,

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:47.280
identifying the instances
that are unhealthy

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.990
and replacing them
automatically.

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:52.050
So stay tuned for the
next episode, where

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:55.320
we will cover how Critter
Junction can setup autohealing

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.480
and keep their users happy.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:59.400
And remember, always
be architecting.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

